    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="9" relname="span">I have noticed that Microsoft products usually have at least three ways to do any function.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="1" relname="elaboration">Usually there is a keystroke sequence, menu item, and icon.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="10" relname="span">Sometimes there are multiple functionalities to do the same task.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="3" relname="elaboration">For instance, in various versions of Windows you can type a text file in either Notepad or Wordpad.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="6" relname="antithesis">Part of this is to maintain backward compatibility as Windows evolves,</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="11" relname="span">but occasionally I get the feeling that these products are so complex that the various groups working on them "reinvent the wheel" and don't realize it.</segment>
        <group id="7" type="span"/>
        <group id="8" type="span" parent="7" relname="span"/>
        <group id="9" type="span" parent="8" relname="span"/>
        <group id="10" type="span" parent="9" relname="elaboration"/>
        <group id="11" type="span" parent="8" relname="nonvolitional-result"/>
    </body>
</rst>
